1.3 Proposed System
The proposed system is an internal university platform for managing and sharing campus resources. Authorized users will be able to register assets, publish surplus items, search for resources, submit requests, manage approvals and transfers, record maintenance activities, and review asset histories. The platform will also provide reports about resource use, costs, savings, and sustainability outcomes.
1.4 Prototype Scope
The prototype will deliver the essential functions identified during Phase 1. Advanced functions may be implemented later if sufficient time and resources remain.
Traditional Functions
The traditional functions include:
•	User authentication and role-based access control.
•	Department and asset management.
•	Publication of surplus or unused assets.
•	Asset search and filtering.
•	Resource requests and status tracking.
•	Approval and transfer workflows.
•	Asset ownership, location, and custody tracking.
•	Maintenance and life-cycle records.
•	Notifications and audit records.
•	Basic financial, operational, and sustainability reports.
Artificial Intelligence Functions
The AI functions include:
•	Classifying assets into suitable categories and tags.
•	Matching available assets with departmental requests.
•	Scoring and ranking suitable matches.
•	Explaining why an asset was recommended.
•	Identifying missing or incomplete asset information.
•	Producing AI-generated summaries and reports.
AI will support users rather than make final decisions. Authorized university staff will approve transfers, repairs, donations, recycling, and disposal.
System exclusions
The semester prototype will not include:
•	Production-wide university deployment or migration of all university records.
•	Live integration with university identity, procurement, finance, maintenance, or enterprise systems.
•	Automatic purchasing, payment, transfer, donation, recycling, or disposal without human approval.
•	Replacement of the university’s official asset register.
•	Autonomous AI decisions or guaranteed recommendation accuracy.
•	IoT or RFID tracking, sensor-based predictive maintenance, or a native mobile application.
•	Production-level backup, monitoring, disaster recovery, security certification, or service-level guarantees.
1.5 Main Stakeholders and Their Needs
Department Representatives and Requesters
They need to publish surplus assets, search for available resources, submit requests, and monitor request progress.
Asset Custodians and Maintenance Staff
They need accurate information about asset condition, location, custody, inspections, repairs, and maintenance history.
University Administrators
They need clear approval workflows, reliable records, and audit trails for important actions.
Procurement and Finance Officers
They need information about available resources, purchasing needs, asset values, costs, and estimated savings.
Sustainability Officers
They need information about reuse, repair, waste reduction, asset-life extension, and estimated environmental benefits.
System Administrators
They need secure tools for managing user accounts, roles, permissions, system settings, and technical logs.
Project Team and Course Staff
The project team needs clear requirements, suitable development tools, sample data, and regular feedback. The instructor and lab engineer need access to project documents, the prototype, testing results, and the GitHub repository.
1.6 Main Assumptions
The project is based on the following assumptions:
•	University departments will be willing to share and reuse resources.
•	Users will provide accurate and updated asset information.
•	Authorized personnel will review and approve important actions.
•	Users will have internet access and basic computer skills.
•	The team will have access to suitable development tools and sample data.
•	AI recommendations will be reviewed by users before decisions are made.
1.7 Main Constraints
Budget
The project must remain within the available student-project budget. The team will prioritize university-provided, open-source, and free-tier tools.
Time
The prototype must be completed within the semester schedule. Essential functions will be prioritized before desirable or advanced features.
Team Size and Skills
The project will be completed by a three-member team. The selected technologies must be manageable using the team’s current technical skills and the available learning time.
Tools and Technology
Git and GitHub must be used for version control and collaboration. The project also requires appropriate development, database, testing, interface-design, diagramming, and AI tools.
Data Access
Access to real university asset records may be restricted because of privacy and confidentiality requirements. Realistic sample data will therefore be used unless official access is provided. The accuracy of AI recommendations and sustainability estimates will depend on the quality and quantity of the available data.
2. Market and Operational Feasibility
2.1 Need for the Proposed System and Interview Findings
Universities manage large numbers of assets, including furniture, computers, laboratory equipment, and office supplies. Some of these assets may remain unused in one department while another department purchases similar resources. When asset information is stored in separate files or communicated through emails, departments may find it difficult to identify available items, coordinate transfers, and maintain accurate records. The proposed system addresses this problem by providing a centralized platform for asset registration, internal sharing, requests, approvals, transfers, maintenance, and reporting.
The proposed system also supports the university’s sustainability objectives. Khalifa University’s 2025 sustainability report identifies green procurement, resource efficiency, and waste reduction as important areas of campus operations. Therefore, a system that supports asset reuse and tracks avoided purchases and waste could contribute to these objectives [10].
The main users would be department representatives, asset custodians, employees requesting resources, university administrators, maintenance teams, procurement staff, finance staff, sustainability officers, and system administrators. Each group would use the system for different activities, but they would share the objective of improving the use and management of university resources.

Interview Summary
The two simulated interviews showed that the proposed system could improve university asset management by centralizing records, helping departments find unused resources, and simplifying requests, approvals, maintenance, and transfers. Stakeholders supported AI for asset classification and matching, but all-important decisions must remain under human control. Key requirements include accurate data, role-based access, clear responsibilities, approval workflows, audit trails, notifications, financial and sustainability reports, staff training, and special handling of restricted assets. These simulated findings must be confirmed through real stakeholder interviews before being presented as actual evidence.
2.2 Comparison with Existing Systems
Three systems relevant to the proposed project are Rheaply, Warp It, and IBM Maximo Application Suite. Rheaply and Warp It support organizational resource reuse, while Maximo focuses on enterprise asset life-cycle and maintenance management. The proposed system combines selected reuse, maintenance, and AI functions in a smaller university-focused prototype.
Rheaply provides centralized asset information, internal reuse, approval workflows, redeployment, audit trails, and sustainability reporting. Its platform is designed to help organizations understand what they own, identify idle resources, and reduce unnecessary purchasing [11].
Warp It is an organizational reuse platform used by universities, government departments, healthcare organizations, and other institutions. It allows users to list surplus resources, search for items, submit claims, manage approvals, and measure financial and environmental outcomes. It also provides an AI-assisted listing function that can suggest an item’s title, category, and value from a photograph [12].
IBM Maximo Application Suite combines asset and facilities management with maintenance, inspections, reliability, and AI-supported decision-making. It is relevant because it demonstrates integrated asset life-cycle and maintenance capabilities, although its enterprise scope and deployment requirements are greater than those of a semester prototype .



