2.3 Evidence of University Adoption
A real example of university adoption is the University of Liverpool’s use of Warp It. The university made the platform available to its staff for listing, requesting, and claiming unwanted furniture, equipment, laboratory resources, and stationery. Shortly after its introduction, the university reported that 263 staff members had registered, 181 items had been exchanged, £27,248 had been saved, and more than three tonnes of waste had been avoided [13].
The same case also demonstrates that operational responsibilities must be assigned clearly. University staff list and claim items, while the Mail and Transport team manages the collection and movement of claimed resources. The university also requires users to obtain authorization before listing an item and excludes IT-issued assets, data-holding devices, confidential items, and hazardous materials from the general reuse process.
This example does not prove that Khalifa University would achieve the same results. However, it provides evidence that university employees can adopt a digital reuse workflow when responsibilities, permissions, transportation, and training support are clearly defined.
2.4 Operational Adoption by Stakeholders
Departments
Departments could adopt the system by assigning one or more representatives to manage their resource listings and requests. Department representatives would identify unused resources, submit requests, update information, and coordinate handovers. Departments would also need to confirm that an item is no longer required before making it available to others.
Asset Custodians
Asset custodians would be responsible for maintaining accurate information about asset ownership, location, condition, availability, and custody. Before a transfer is completed, the custodian would confirm that the item exists, is suitable for transfer, and has been correctly described. Custodians would also record the handover and update the asset’s location and responsible department.
University Administrators
Administrators would review requests and approve controlled actions. The exact approval level may depend on the type, value, condition, or sensitivity of an asset. High-value assets, disposal decisions, donations, and transfers involving restricted equipment may require additional approval.
Maintenance Teams
Maintenance staff could use the system to receive inspection or repair requests, review asset histories, record faults, update repair progress, and confirm whether an item is ready for reuse. This workflow would replace or supplement separate maintenance records. Integration with an existing maintenance system may be required if the university already uses one.
Procurement Staff
Procurement staff could check whether an appropriate reusable asset is available before approving a new purchase. They could also review unsuccessful requests to identify resources that still need to be purchased. This would make reuse part of the procurement process rather than an optional activity completed after a purchase request has already been approved.
Sustainability Officers
Sustainability officers could review information about reused items, repaired assets, avoided purchases, waste diversion, and estimated carbon-emission reductions. They would also be responsible for reviewing the assumptions and calculation methods used in sustainability reports.
Overall, these workflows appear operationally feasible because they are connected to activities that these stakeholder groups already perform. The system would mainly centralize the information and improve coordination between them. However, the proposed roles and approval levels would need to be validated with actual university representatives before implementation.
