•	Five to eight users from each participating department: 15–24 departmental users
•	Eight to twelve central users from administration, procurement, maintenance, sustainability, finance, and IT
•	Estimated total pilot population: 23–36 users
A small pilot would allow the university to test the workflows, approval rules, data fields, training materials, and AI recommendations before considering a wider rollout. The actual number of users and departments would need to be confirmed by the university.
2.8 Possible Adoption Difficulties
The main adoption difficulties may include:
•	Departments may continue using email or informal communication.
•	Employees may consider asset registration an additional workload.
•	Asset information may be incomplete, inaccurate, or outdated.
•	Departments may be unwilling to release assets that they may need later.
•	Approval responsibilities may be unclear or require several management levels.
•	The system may duplicate functions available in existing procurement or maintenance systems.
•	Users may not trust AI recommendations or understand their explanations.
•	Restricted, confidential, hazardous, or data-holding equipment may require separate procedures.
•	Environmental estimates may be unreliable if cost, weight, material, or carbon data are unavailable.
•	Some users may require additional training or technical support.
These difficulties could be reduced through a limited pilot, clear responsibility assignments, simple data-entry forms, management support, role-based training, written approval rules, and integration with existing university procedures. The system should also provide normal search and request functions when its AI services are unavailable.
2.9 Feasibility Conclusion
The proposed system is operationally feasible if the university clearly assigns responsibilities, defines approval levels, provides training, and introduces the system gradually. Existing systems such as Rheaply and Warp It demonstrate that internal resource reuse and sustainability reporting are practical organizational functions. The University of Liverpool case further shows that university employees can adopt similar workflows and achieve measurable reuse outcomes.
The proposed system offers additional value by combining internal exchange with maintenance tracking, complete asset histories, sustainability reporting, and AI-supported classification and matching. A pilot involving approximately three departments and 23–36 users would provide a practical way to evaluate adoption before a university-wide implementation.
3. Technical Feasibility
3.1 Proposed Technical Approach
The proposed system can be developed as a web-based application using a three-layer architecture. The presentation layer will provide the user interface, the application layer will manage business rules and communication with external services, and the data layer will store asset, user, request, maintenance, and reporting information.
Web architecture is suitable because users from different university departments can access the system through a browser without installing separate desktop software. It also allows the development team to update the prototype from one central location.
The proposed prototype will use Next.js and TypeScript for the user interface and application logic. Supabase will provide a PostgreSQL database, authentication, file storage, and application programming interfaces. PostgreSQL can store the structured system records, while the pgvector extension can store numerical embeddings used for semantic search. An external AI service, such as the OpenAI API, can provide asset classification, description generation, semantic embeddings, recommendation explanations, and report summaries.
