3.3 Architecture and Integration Feasibility
The frontend will communicate with the database and backend functions through secure APIs. Users will sign in before accessing system functions, and role-based rules will determine which records and actions they can access. Asset photographs and documents will be stored separately from the structured database records.
The main integrations required for the prototype are:
•	Authentication and user-role management
•	Database and file-storage access
•	AI classification and text-generation requests
•	Embedding generation for semantic search
•	Email or in-system notifications
•	Exporting reports in formats such as CSV or PDF
•	GitHub integration for deployment and version control
These integrations are technically achievable through documented APIs. However, integration with existing university procurement, maintenance, finance, or identity-management systems should not be assumed during the prototype. Such integration would require technical documentation, test accounts, security approval, and cooperation from university IT staff. The prototype should therefore use its own controlled workflows and sample data while keeping the interfaces modular enough for future integration.
3.4 AI and Semantic Search Feasibility
The proposed technology can support the required AI functions. Asset information and images can be sent to an AI service to suggest categories, tags, and standardized descriptions. The system can then present these suggestions to an authorized user for review before saving them.
Semantic search can be implemented by converting asset descriptions and resource requests into embeddings. The embeddings can be stored in PostgreSQL using pgvector and compared using cosine distance. Supabase documents the use of HNSW indexes for approximate nearest-neighbor searches over high-dimensional vectors. Its current documentation states that the standard vector type can be indexed up to 2,000 dimensions, which is sufficient for many embedding models [7].
The matching process can combine semantic similarity with structured information such as category, quantity, condition, location, urgency, and availability. The system can then calculate a combined score and present the highest-ranked assets. AI-generated explanations may summarize the main reasons for each recommendation, but they should not be treated as final decisions.
AI feasibility is subject to several limitations. API services require a reliable internet connection and may impose account-specific rate limits. Service prices and limits may also change. AI outputs may be incomplete or incorrect, particularly when asset descriptions or photographs are unclear. The system should therefore validate inputs, display confidence or matching information where possible, record the model used, and allow users to correct or reject every AI recommendation.
3.5 Reporting Feasibility
The database can support operational reports about assets, requests, approvals, transfers, maintenance activities, and status changes. Financial reports can use recorded purchase values, repair costs, transfer costs, and avoided-purchase estimates. Sustainability reports can use information such as item quantity, weight, reuse status, repair status, and estimated carbon factors.
Basic reports can be generated using SQL queries and displayed with a JavaScript chart library. AI may be used to produce short written summaries, but the numerical results should be calculated using defined formulas rather than generated directly by the AI model.
The main reporting limitation is data quality. Financial savings and environmental benefits cannot be calculated accurately when asset values, weights, material types, or conversion factors are missing. The prototype should clearly label estimates and store the source, formula, and assumptions used for each sustainability calculation.
3.6 Hosting and Service Limits
The prototype can use Supabase’s free plan for the database, authentication, and file storage. As of September 2026, the free plan includes:
•	500 MB of database storage
•	1 GB of file storage
•	5 GB of standard egress
•	5 GB of cached egress
•	50,000 monthly active users
•	A maximum file-upload size of 50 MB
•	500,000 Edge Function invocations
•	Pausing after one week of inactivity
These limits are more than sufficient for a small student prototype, but the lack of automatic backups, the one-day log-retention period, and project pausing make the free plan unsuitable for a production university system [7].
The frontend can be hosted on Vercel’s Hobby plan. Vercel currently limits Hobby accounts to 100 deployments per day, 100 MB of static source-file uploads per deployment, one concurrent deployment, and one hour of runtime-log retention. The platform also applies a 120-second timeout to proxied requests [15].These limits are acceptable for a prototype but may affect long-running AI requests, large uploads, or detailed operational monitoring.
AI API rate limits depend on the selected model and the account’s usage tier. Therefore, the development team should check the current limits in the service dashboard, restrict unnecessary calls, cache generated results, and handle rate-limit errors. The prototype should also preserve its normal search, request, approval, and reporting functions when the AI service is unavailable.
