The proposed stack does not require specialized servers or high-performance local hardware because the database, storage, and AI processing can be hosted externally. A laptop with at least 8 GB of RAM, a modern multi-core processor, and several gigabytes of free disk space should be sufficient for normal development. This is a planning recommendation rather than a strict platform requirement.
3.10 Major Technical Limitations
The main technical limitations are:
•	The free hosting plans are intended for prototypes rather than production systems.
•	AI services require internet access and may create unpredictable costs.
•	API rate limits or service outages may temporarily disable AI functions.
•	Limited access to university systems may prevent real integration.
•	Sample data may not represent the quality or scale of real university records.
•	Semantic matching quality depends on accurate asset descriptions and requests.
•	Image classification may be unreliable when photographs are unclear.
•	Sustainability calculations depend on external data and documented assumptions.
•	The team has limited time to learn several new technologies.
•	A three-member team may not be able to implement every proposed function fully.
•	Production deployment would require stronger backup, monitoring, security, privacy, and availability controls.
These limitations can be reduced by prioritizing essential workflows, using modular components, compressing uploaded images, caching AI results, maintaining non-AI fallback functions, and testing integrations early.
3.11 Technical Feasibility Conclusion
The proposed system is technically feasible as a student prototype. A web application using Next.js, TypeScript, Supabase, PostgreSQL, pgvector, Vercel, GitHub, and an external AI API can support the required asset workflows, semantic search, AI assistance, reporting, and basic integrations.
The proposed free hosting services provide sufficient capacity for the 500-asset storage scenario in Section 3.7 and approximately 23–36 pilot users. The 500-asset figure is a planning scenario, not the formal evaluation dataset or a confirmed university deployment size. The free plans would not be suitable for a full university deployment because of storage, backup, monitoring, reliability, security, and support limitations.
The greatest technical risks are the team’s learning requirements, integration complexity, AI reliability, limited access to university systems, and the available semester time. The project should therefore implement traditional asset-management functions first and add AI features gradually after the core workflows are stable.




